WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:06.170
Fun Fact: One of the Marvel Cinematic Universe's
most powerful Phase 3 heroes nearly popped

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:09.920
up in Avengers: Age of Ultron…well, sort
of!

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:15.210
According to a proud owner of the pricey Infinity
Saga box set, a deleted scene illustrates

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:20.880
an interesting tidbit disclosed by Marvel
Studios head honcho Kevin Feige back in 2015:

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.410
that Carol Danvers, AKA Captain Marvel, was
almost introduced at the conclusion of that

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:27.410
film.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:32.210
You may recall that Age of Ultron ended with
Steve Rogers and Natasha Romanoff, aka Captain

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:37.320
America and Black Widow, assembling a new
Avengers team; Tony Stark was taking a breather,

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.310
Bruce Banner was M.I.A., and Thor was off-planet
investigating the Infinity Stones.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:47.900
Gathered at the Avengers HQ was James Rhodes
in his War Machine armor, Sam Wilson, aka

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:54.100
the Falcon, and new allies Vision and Wanda
Maximoff, also known as Scarlet Witch.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.610
The latter made her entrance by flying into
the facility, and eagle-eyed viewers noticed

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:03.600
that something about Maximoff looked just
a little bit...off in that scene.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.620
That's because the special effects shot wasn't
designed for her, but for Danvers, who had

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:13.600
yet to be cast, and the subsequent shot of
the new team assembled before Rogers and Romanoff

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.490
was filmed with a stand-in for Captain Marvel
front and center, one who was subsequently

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:23.640
digitally removed from the scene, as pointed
out by a Reddit user who owns the box set.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:29.260
The unaltered shot is finally available for
fans to "oooh" and "aaah" over on the recently-released

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:34.410
box set, but Feige went public with its existence
shortly after Age of Ultron's release.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:39.590
Speaking with Birth.Movies.Death in 2015,
Feige explained that while the good Captain's

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:44.490
inclusion was actually part of an early draft
of the screenplay, he was never exactly on

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:45.600
board with it.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:46.710
Feige explained:

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.760
"[Captain Marvel] was in a draft [of Age of
Ultron].

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.191
But to me, it would have done that character
a disservice, to meet her fully formed, in

00:01:54.191 --> 00:01:59.600
a costume and part of the Avengers already,
when 99% of the audience would go, 'Who is

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:00.600
that?'

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:01.910
It's just not the way we've done it before."

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:06.500
"You have no idea who I am.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.970
I don't even know who I am!"

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.430
Feige went on to recall how he essentially
humored director Joss Whedon, who had gotten

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:17.720
it into his head that introducing an iconic,
never-before-seen character during Age of

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.910
Ultron's final seconds somehow wouldn't have
confused the hell out of the audience.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:23.290
Feige went on:

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:27.469
"The way we reveal Scarlet Witch [in costume]
at the end of the movie?

00:02:27.469 --> 00:02:29.260
Those were Captain Marvel plate shots.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.209
[Whedon] said, 'We'll cast her later!'

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:34.510
And I said, 'Yeah Joss, we'll cast her later.'"

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:39.420
Feige then pantomimed whispering to someone
else, adding, quote, "We're not putting her

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:40.420
in there!"

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.260
Fortunately, Whedon eventually saw it Feige's
way, and the King Geek explained that it was

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:48.590
actually the director's idea to reuse those
plate shots for Maximoff's entrance.

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:49.849
Feige dished:

00:02:49.849 --> 00:02:54.610
"Finally, Joss was like, 'Let's use those
plates to let Scarlet Witch fly into frame,

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:55.930
give her a big entrance.'

00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:00.150
And that makes sense, she's come to their
side, and she deserves the cool intro, which

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:03.349
will feed into another movie we start shooting
in a few weeks."

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:07.609
The movie to which he was referring, of course,
was Captain America: Civil War.

00:03:07.609 --> 00:03:12.230
That flick's first act hinged on a terrible
accident involving Maximoff that prompted

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:16.949
the world's government to fast-track the adoption
of the Sokovia Accords, which required all

00:03:16.949 --> 00:03:20.549
superheroes to essentially be registered government
agents.

00:03:20.549 --> 00:03:25.069
Whether or not to fall in line caused a serious
rift among the Avengers, leading to the titular

00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:29.559
conflict, a conflict which, it's safe to say,
would have gone a heck of a lot differently

00:03:29.559 --> 00:03:31.599
had Captain Marvel been involved.

00:03:31.599 --> 00:03:36.799
Whedon may have eventually remembered that
giving out-of-left-field, unheralded introductions

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:42.159
to iconic heroes isn't exactly how the MCU
has historically gotten down, but before he

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:47.500
did, he had designs on introducing another
Marvel hero in that final scene, as well.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.860
He may even have actually done it, had the
deal between Marvel Studios and Sony Pictures

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:55.859
to share custody of Spider-Man been reached
just a little bit sooner.

00:03:55.859 --> 00:04:00.680
Shortly after Age of Ultron's release, Whedon
visited the Empire Film podcast to talk about

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.969
the picture, and he casually disclosed that
if he had gotten his way, ol' web-head would

00:04:04.969 --> 00:04:09.349
have also inexplicably appeared alongside
the Avengers in that final scene.

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:11.549
According to Slashfilm, Whedon explained:

00:04:11.549 --> 00:04:16.229
"I said, it would be great if we could just
add a few more, if we could have a Captain

00:04:16.229 --> 00:04:18.340
Marvel there...And they talked about it.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:23.090
I was like, 'And Spider-Man, we could do that
too,' because Sony had approached us during

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:26.630
[the production of the first Avengers movie]
about a little integration.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:30.500
So I would have put both of them in, but neither
of the deals were made.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:34.980
And then they were like, 'We're making a Captain
Marvel movie and we've got Spider-Man as a

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.220
property,' and I'm like, 'I've already locked
my film, you f---ers!

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:40.520
Thanks for nothing.'"

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:45.220
Personally, we're glad the friendly neighborhood
Spider-Man swung into the MCU when he did,

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.230
but it's hard not to wonder how things would
have panned out had Peter Parker leapt into

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.640
the fray just a little bit sooner!

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.220
"Hi, I'm Peter Parker."

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.110
"Hey, Peter Parker."

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:01.350
The Infinity Saga box set has been released,
but good luck getting your hands on one at

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:08.820
the suggested retail price of $549.99; the
first shipment sold out almost immediately,

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.850
and they're currently going on eBay for close
to twice that.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:16.090
Hopefully, Marvel and Disney will get more
on retailers' shelves in plenty of time for

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:17.110
the holidays.

